        "signature": "",
        "thinking": "The issue is clear. `createHyperdriveDb` creates a `postgres` client with `max: 5`. This creates a connection pool. When the first request comes in, the pool makes a TCP connection. When the second request comes in and uses the same pool, CF Workers says \"can't use I/O from request 1 in request 2.\"\n\nThe fix: create a new `postgres` client per request (not cached). In production with Hyperdrive, this is fine because Hyperdrive handles connection pooling on the server side.\n\nI need to change `buildRouteCache` to NOT cache the DB — only cache the routes (compiled routes) and create the DB fresh per request.\n\n\nSo the route cache should hold just the stateless route definitions and their compiled regexps, while the postgres client gets instantiated fresh for each request since it creates TCP connections. The R2 storage binding should be safe to cache since it's just a reference to the CF-managed resource, not a socket.",
        "type": "thinking"
      }
